zoe.devex: FYI the sample in samples/async-readme.md uses a top-level await snippet that breaks for folks on Node 14 and CommonJS installs. oliver.newhire just hit it during onboarding and lost time.

oliver.newhire: paste i ran (copy/paste from README):

```js
// README example (top-level await)
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY });
const resp = await client.create_completion({ prompt: 'hi' });
console.log(resp.output_text);
```

oliver.newhire: error in my terminal: `SyntaxError: await is only valid in async function` and `SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'` when folks use CommonJS. confused why copy/paste failed.

hank.engineer: yep—two drifts here: 1) README assumes ESM + Node 16+ (top-level await), 2) package.json in the sample pins `"type": "commonjs"` and `engines.node` to `>=14`. so the example and the repo metadata are out of sync.

zoe.devex: thanks. this is a classic copy/paste trap for new hires. can we fix README to show two variants (ESM with top-level await + CJS async IIFE) and bump package metadata?

sana.docs: proposed README additions: include the exact failing snippet and two replacement snippets. also add a "Common mistakes" callout with the error text so support searches find it quickly.

sana.docs: ESM / Node16+ example: 

```js
// ESM (Node 16+, package.json { "type": "module" })
import RedwoodClient from '@redwood/sdk';
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY });
const resp = await client.create_completion({ prompt: 'hi' });
console.log(resp.output_text);
```

nsana.docs: CommonJS-compatible replacement (works on Node 14+):

```js
// CommonJS (works with older setups)
const RedwoodClient = require('@redwood/sdk');
(async () => {
  const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY });
  const resp = await client.create_completion({ prompt: 'hi' });
  console.log(resp.output_text);
})();
```

hank.engineer: also update samples/async/package.json: set `"type": "module"` if we want to show ESM by default, bump `engines.node` to `>=16` and update README header to say "Choose the snippet that matches your environment." Alternatively keep CommonJS code path and make ESM an optional example. which do we prefer?

zoe.devex: prefer showing both and setting the repo to CommonJS to avoid surprising folks on older workstations. add a short migration note: "If you copied the top-level await example and saw `SyntaxError: await is only valid in async function`, either switch to the CommonJS IIFE snippet or upgrade Node / set `type: 'module'` in package.json."

sana.docs: i'll open a PR that: updates README with both snippets + "Common mistakes" block (includes the exact errors: `SyntaxError: await is only valid in async function` and `SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'`), leaves package.json as CommonJS but bumps `@redwood/sdk` to `^1.4.0`, and adds a tiny smoke test `samples/async/test/smoke.js` that runs both snippets against a local stub and asserts they print something.

ci-bot: PR #1034 created by sana.docs  CI running smoke-check. :gear:

hank.engineer: added a small GH Actions matrix job that runs smoke.js on ubuntu-latest with Node 14 and Node 18 to ensure both code paths keep working. cheap guardrail.

oliver.newhire: thanks  the README updates made it very clear what to paste for my setup. the error snippet was especially helpful.

sana.docs: merged and cut samples v0.8.0. posted a short note in #onboarding reminding mentors to verify local node versions for cohorts. also added the error text to the docs-searchable common-pitfalls page.

zoe.devex: great work everyone. small README + CI smoke additions like this dramatically reduce onboarding friction. :tada:
